explainer "AIPerf vs Locust" {
  id: "aiperf-vs-locust"
  route: "/aiperf-vs-locust"
  topic: "architecture"
  storagePrefix: "aiperf-vs-locust-explainer"
  classPrefix: "deck-aiperf-vs-locust"
  eyebrowLabel: "AIPERF VS LOCUST"
  startGateTitle: "Two load-generation architectures, traced from source"

  hub: {
    highlight: "aiperf vs locust"
    title: "gevent greenlets + ZeroMQ vs multiprocessing + ZMQ credit routing"
    description: "Locust simulates virtual users as cooperative gevent greenlets coordinated over ZeroMQ. AIPerf runs every worker as a real OS process, admitted per-request by a ZMQ-routed credit ledger."
  }

  slide "Two architectures" {
    eyebrow: "Overview"
    title: "Cooperative greenlets vs process-per-worker credit routing"
    lede: "Locust's unit of concurrency is a gevent greenlet running one User's task loop. AIPerf's unit of concurrency is a real OS process admitted into flight by a credit dispatched over a ZeroMQ ROUTER socket."

    narration: "Both tools are built around message-passing infrastructure — Locust uses ZeroMQ for its master/worker wire protocol, and AIPerf uses ZeroMQ for essentially everything between its many processes. But what rides on top of that infrastructure is structurally different. Locust's concurrency unit is a greenlet: cheap, cooperatively scheduled, no OS thread or process backing it. AIPerf's concurrency unit is a real multiprocessing.Process, and every request that process sends is individually authorized by a credit dispatched from a central router. The rest of this deck traces both all the way from source."

    term: {
      word: "concurrency unit"
      meaning: "The thing a load-testing tool multiplies to reach a target in-flight-request count. Locust multiplies gevent greenlets inside worker processes; AIPerf multiplies real worker processes, each admitted per-request by a credit."
    }

    points: [
      "Locust: User class → gevent.Greenlet, many per OS process, ZeroMQ ROUTER/DEALER only for master↔worker coordination.",
      "AIPerf: every service (worker, timing_manager, dataset_manager, records_manager, ...) is its own multiprocessing.Process.",
      "AIPerf's ZMQ usage is pervasive: an event bus, a credit router, a credit-return channel, and two more proxies, all inside one run.",
      "Evidence: locust/user/users.py, locust/rpc/zmqrpc.py; aiperf/controller/multiprocess_service_manager.py, aiperf/common/enums/enums.py (CommAddress)."
    ]

    caption: "Greenlets inside processes vs processes admitted by credits."

    render: @scene {
      sdk.Header(
        id = "s1-hdr",
        title = "GREENLETS vs CREDIT-ROUTED PROCESSES",
        caption = "Greenlets inside processes vs processes admitted by credits.",
        x = 18, y = 16, width = 664, height = 44,
        surfaceRole = theme(surface.secondary),
        inkRole = theme(ink.secondary)
      )
      sdk.Panel(
        id = "s1-locust",
        title = "Locust",
        detail = "User → gevent.Greenlet",
        x = 60, y = 110, width = 240, height = 100,
        surfaceRole = theme(surface.elevated),
        strokeRole = theme(accent.secondary)
      )
      sdk.Panel(
        id = "s1-aiperf",
        title = "AIPerf",
        detail = "Credit → worker OS process",
        x = 400, y = 110, width = 240, height = 100,
        surfaceRole = theme(surface.elevated),
        strokeRole = theme(accent.primary)
      )
      sdk.Divider(
        id = "s1-div",
        axis = "y",
        x = 350, y = 90, length = 220,
        strokeRole = theme(ink.tertiary)
      )
      sdk.Note(
        id = "s1-note",
        text = "Both use ZeroMQ — for different jobs and at different scope",
        x = 60, y = 250, width = 580, height = 30
      )
      sdk.Signal(
        id = "s1-motion",
        from = { nodeId: "s1-locust", anchor: "e" },
        to = { nodeId: "s1-aiperf", anchor: "w" },
        opacity = 0.45,
        strokeWidth = 2.2,
        label = "two architectures",
        style = { stroke: theme(accent.tertiary) }
      )

      timeline main {
        at 0 reveal s1-hdr duration 260
        after s1-hdr reveal s1-locust duration 280
        after s1-locust reveal s1-div duration 220
        after s1-div reveal s1-aiperf duration 280
        after s1-aiperf reveal s1-note duration 260
        after s1-note trace s1-motion duration 900
      }
    }
  }

  slide "Locust: the per-user loop" {
    eyebrow: "Locust architecture"
    title: "TaskSet.run(): execute a task, then wait(), forever"
    lede: "User.start() spawns group.spawn(run_user, self) — a real gevent.Greenlet. Inside it, TaskSet.run() loops: pick a task, execute it, call wait(). With wait_time=0, wait() reduces to gevent.sleep(0), a cooperative yield with no time delay."

    narration: "The actual loop lives in TaskSet.run(): while True, pop a task off the queue if empty, execute it via execute_next_task, then call self.wait(). wait() calls self._sleep(self.wait_time()), and _sleep is literally gevent.sleep(seconds) — no other primitive is used. When a User subclass sets wait_time to constant(0), that lambda always returns the integer 0, so the loop calls gevent.sleep(0). Per gevent's own semantics, sleep(0) does not block for any wall-clock duration — it performs exactly one cooperative context-switch to the event loop, letting other ready greenlets run, then resumes. So the effective behavior is: fire a request, and the instant it completes, loop back and fire the next one, rate-limited only by request latency and gevent's scheduling fairness, not by any artificial delay."

    term: {
      word: "gevent.sleep(0)"
      meaning: "gevent's primitive for yielding cooperative control back to the event loop without a wall-clock delay. Used as the wait() implementation when wait_time returns 0, making a Locust User's loop effectively continuous rather than throttled."
    }

    points: [
      "User.start(group) → group.spawn(run_user, self) — locust/user/users.py:200, a real gevent.Greenlet.",
      "TaskSet.run() loop: get_next_task → execute_next_task → wait() — locust/user/task.py:354-368.",
      "wait() → self._sleep(self.wait_time()); _sleep is gevent.sleep(seconds) — task.py:451-470.",
      "User.wait_time = constant(0) by default; constant(0) is `lambda instance: 0` — users.py:82, wait_time.py:18-27."
    ]

    caption: "One greenlet, one loop: execute, yield, execute again."

    render: @scene {
      sdk.Header(
        id = "s2-hdr",
        title = "TaskSet.run() — THE ACTUAL LOOP",
        caption = "One greenlet, one loop: execute, yield, execute again.",
        x = 18, y = 16, width = 664, height = 44,
        surfaceRole = theme(surface.secondary),
        inkRole = theme(ink.secondary)
      )
      sdk.Panel(
        id = "s2-exec",
        title = "execute_next_task()",
        detail = "runs the @task method",
        x = 80, y = 100, width = 220, height = 80,
        surfaceRole = theme(surface.elevated),
        strokeRole = theme(accent.primary)
      )
      sdk.Edge(
        id = "s2-e1",
        mode = "connector",
        from = { nodeId: "s2-exec", anchor: "e" },
        to = { nodeId: "s2-wait", anchor: "w" },
        style = { stroke: theme(accent.tertiary), strokeWidth: 2.2 }
      )
      sdk.Panel(
        id = "s2-wait",
        title = "wait()",
        detail = "gevent.sleep(wait_time())",
        x = 380, y = 100, width = 220, height = 80,
        surfaceRole = theme(surface.elevated),
        strokeRole = theme(accent.tertiary)
      )
      sdk.Edge(
        id = "s2-loop",
        mode = "route",
        from = { nodeId: "s2-wait", anchor: "s" },
        to = { nodeId: "s2-exec", anchor: "s" },
        style = { stroke: theme(accent.tertiary), strokeWidth: 2, dash: [4, 3] }
      )
      sdk.Panel(
        id = "s2-zero",
        title = "wait_time = constant(0)",
        detail = "gevent.sleep(0) → cooperative yield, 0 delay",
        x = 160, y = 240, width = 400, height = 70,
        surfaceRole = theme(surface.elevated),
        strokeRole = theme(accent.green)
      )
      sdk.Edge(
        id = "s2-e2",
        mode = "route",
        from = { nodeId: "s2-wait", anchor: "s" },
        to = { nodeId: "s2-zero", anchor: "n" },
        style = { stroke: theme(accent.green), strokeWidth: 2 }
      )
      sdk.Note(
        id = "s2-note",
        text = "No other delay primitive exists in this loop — _sleep is only ever gevent.sleep()",
        x = 60, y = 335, width = 580, height = 30
      )
      sdk.Signal(
        id = "s2-motion",
        from = { nodeId: "s2-exec", anchor: "center" },
        to = { nodeId: "s2-wait", anchor: "center" },
        opacity = 0.45,
        strokeWidth = 2.2,
        label = "continuous loop",
        style = { stroke: theme(accent.primary) }
      )

      timeline main {
        at 0 reveal s2-hdr duration 260
        after s2-hdr reveal s2-exec duration 260
        after s2-exec trace s2-e1 duration 280
        after s2-e1 reveal s2-wait duration 260
