    }
  }

  slide "AIPerf: process topology" {
    eyebrow: "AIPerf architecture"
    title: "system_controller launches and hosts everything else"
    lede: "The aiperf profile CLI process becomes the SystemController, which starts three ZMQ proxies inside itself, then forks dataset_manager, timing_manager, worker_manager, and records_manager as real OS processes — and workers are spawned dynamically, on command, after the run starts."

    narration: "SystemController is where the aiperf profile invocation actually lands — it hosts the ZMQ proxies directly (not as separate processes) and, via a MultiProcessServiceManager, forks every other required service as a genuine multiprocessing.Process: dataset_manager, timing_manager, worker_manager, records_manager always, plus optional gpu_telemetry_manager, server_metrics_manager, network_latency_manager, and an API server depending on config. Workers themselves are not part of that initial required set — they're spawned dynamically in response to a SpawnWorkersCommand, which WorkerManager computes and emits after doing its own CPU-count-based sizing, and SystemController is the one that actually calls multiprocessing.Process(...).start() for each worker replica. Record processors scale along with workers automatically, at a quarter of the worker count by default."

    term: {
      word: "SystemController"
      meaning: "The top-level AIPerf process — what an `aiperf profile` invocation actually becomes. It hosts the run's three ZMQ proxies in-process and owns the MultiProcessServiceManager that forks every other service as a real OS process, including workers, which it spawns dynamically on command rather than at startup."
    }

    points: [
      "`aiperf profile` process becomes SystemController; ProxyManager (3 proxies) runs inside it, not as a separate process.",
      "Always-forked: dataset_manager, timing_manager, worker_manager, records_manager — one multiprocessing.Process each.",
      "Workers are spawned dynamically via SpawnWorkersCommand, not at startup — SystemController does the actual Process(...).start().",
      "Record processors auto-scale at max(1, workers // 4) by default (Environment.RECORD.PROCESSOR_SCALE_FACTOR)."
    ]

    caption: "One process hosts the proxies and forks the rest — workers spawn on command."

    render: @scene {
      sdk.Header(
        id = "s5-hdr",
        title = "SYSTEM_CONTROLLER FORKS THE FLEET",
