  slide "AIPerf: the credit lifecycle" {
    eyebrow: "AIPerf architecture"
    title: "Created, dispatched, gated, stuck to a session, returned"
    lede: "CreditIssuer builds a Credit and hands it to StickyCreditRouter, which won't dispatch anything until every worker has announced WorkerReady. Multi-turn conversations stick to the worker that handled the first turn, so session state and cache locality survive the whole conversation."

    narration: "A credit's life starts in CreditIssuer, which acquires a concurrency slot, increments the atomic sent counter, then builds the Credit object and hands it to the router. Before any of that can happen, the phase runner has already awaited wait_for_workers with a 30-second timeout — a startup gate that exists specifically to close the race where a credit could be issued before any worker has registered. Each worker announces itself once, on start, by sending WorkerReady over its DEALER socket; the router treats that as the trigger to register the worker and, on the first one, to unblock anyone waiting on worker availability. Dispatch itself is where the file's name comes from: StickyCreditRouter keeps a session-id-to-worker-id map, and if a credit belongs to a conversation whose earlier turn already landed on a specific worker, it's routed there again — so that worker's cached session state, and any inference-server-side prefix cache locality that follows from hitting the same backend repeatedly, both stay warm across the whole conversation. Only a conversation's first turn goes through least-loaded selection; the sticky mapping is created only if more turns are coming, and is torn down on the conversation's final turn. When a worker finishes, it returns the credit over a completely separate PUSH/PULL channel — not back over the ROUTER — specifically so the dispatch direction never has to double as the return path."

    term: {
      word: "sticky session routing"
      meaning: "StickyCreditRouter's policy of routing every subsequent turn of a multi-turn conversation to the same worker that handled its first turn, keyed by x_correlation_id, so per-conversation session state and any resulting inference-server cache locality persist for the conversation's duration. Only first turns use least-loaded worker selection."
    }

    points: [
      "wait_for_workers(timeout=30s) blocks the very first credit issuance until at least one WorkerReady has been seen.",
      "First turn of a conversation: least-loaded worker selection. Later turns: routed to the same worker via a sticky session map.",
      "Sticky mapping is created only when more turns are coming, and popped on the conversation's final turn.",
      "Credit dispatch (ROUTER→DEALER) and credit return (worker PUSH→router PULL) are separate one-directional channels."
    ]

    caption: "First turn picks the worker; every later turn goes back to the same one."

    render: @scene {
      sdk.Header(
        id = "s7-hdr",
        title = "CREATE → GATE → STICK → RETURN",
        caption = "First turn picks the worker; every later turn goes back to the same one.",
        x = 18, y = 16, width = 664, height = 44,
        surfaceRole = theme(surface.secondary),
        inkRole = theme(ink.secondary)
      )
      sdk.Stepper(
        id = "s7-steps",
        steps = ["CreditIssuer creates", "wait_for_workers gate", "sticky or least-loaded", "PUSH/PULL return"],
        linked = true,
        gap = 14,
        x = 40, y = 70, width = 600, height = 26
      )
      sdk.Panel(
        id = "s7-t1",
        title = "Turn 1",
        detail = "least-loaded worker",
        x = 60, y = 130, width = 180, height = 80,
        surfaceRole = theme(surface.elevated),
        strokeRole = theme(accent.primary)
      )
      sdk.Panel(
        id = "s7-worker",
        title = "worker_a1",
        detail = "sticky mapping created",
        x = 300, y = 130, width = 200, height = 80,
        surfaceRole = theme(surface.elevated),
        strokeRole = theme(accent.warning)
      )
      sdk.Edge(
        id = "s7-e1",
        mode = "connector",
        from = { nodeId: "s7-t1", anchor: "e" },
        to = { nodeId: "s7-worker", anchor: "w" },
        style = { stroke: theme(accent.primary), strokeWidth: 2.2 }
      )
      sdk.Panel(
        id = "s7-t2",
        title = "Turn 2, Turn 3, ...",
        detail = "same worker, sticky routed",
        x = 300, y = 250, width = 200, height = 80,
        surfaceRole = theme(surface.elevated),
        strokeRole = theme(accent.warning)
      )
      sdk.Edge(
        id = "s7-e2",
        mode = "route",
        from = { nodeId: "s7-worker", anchor: "s" },
